<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Corduene</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 384 CE (approx.) · capital Pinaka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain kingdom south of Lake Van that lay on the fault line between Rome and Iran, changing hands in their treaties for four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Corduene (Gordyene) occupied the rugged mountains of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Cizre</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-bohtan">Botan</a> region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> met its warlike inhabitants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carduchi">Carduchi</a>, in 401 BCE. A kingdom appears in the 1st century BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> annexed it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers were local kings or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> who depended on stronger neighbours, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> records that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zarbienus">Zarbienus</a> negotiated secretly with Rome and was put to death by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucullus</a> honoured his memory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread there in late antiquity. The name has often been linked with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, but the connection is uncertain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> in 298 Corduene passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>; in 363 Jovian returned it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, which absorbed it as a province, with its autonomy fading by the late 4th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/corduene.jpg" alt="Corduene" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Willian Shepherd (1871-1934)[2] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_East_ancient_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 384 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pinaka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zarbienus, Jovinianus</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gordyene, Kingdom of Corduene</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=142&amp;focus=corduene" aria-label="Corduene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5764 827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Corduene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6084 1083L6141 1232M6084 1083L5304 942M6084 1083L6357 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6141 1232h0M5304 942h0M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6084 1083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6084 1083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=142&amp;focus=corduene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

